Z||000000||bcibob670||07-16-2000||02:55 PM||||this is my first time posting so ill try not to be too long winded.    I have 86 fiero SE w/ 4cyl., auto trans , have just finished replacing transmission (previous year replaced engine),   most sensors and major electrical components (distributor stuff, wires plugs , alternator), cooling hoses, water pump, belt replaced with in the year. <BR>I have removed the a/c unit, belts in engine compartment.  the fuel filter has been replaced.  <P>The engine runs fine at start up and continues for about an hour and half of normal city traffic doing errands no remarkable symptoms until engine just cuts out (no sputtering just OFF)  after waiting 30 minutes the engine will start again and run for a short time  proportional to the amount of down time.  I AM THINKING IT MAY BE THE FUEL PUMP OVER HEATING OR  ONE OF THE SENSORS MAY NEED A HEAT SHROUD. <P>Please any ideas would be useful THANK YOU ||||reg||9
Z||000001||contra||07-16-2000||05:48 PM||||yeah<BR>i'm no expert on these things, but it sounds like something is overheating in the engine. hopefully somebody else can expand on that<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||07-16-2000||06:23 PM||||Don't think the fuel pump is overheating; otherwise, you'd probably hear it whining at you.  <P>Ignition problem would be my first guess. Like the ignition module (located in the distributor) or ignition coil.  Both of these items have been known to fail due to heat, so I hear.  And they are fairly easy to get to/ replace.<P>If you can't afford the guess-and-try technique, the ignition coil can be tested with an ohmmeter.<P>If you are one of those who <B>have</B> to verify things - carry along an extra spark plug with a grounding clip.  Next time it happens, pull a plug wire off the engine, attach it to your "extra" plug, clip the spark plug to a decent ground, and observe while somebody cranks the starter.  No spark means it's ignition problem, of course.  <P>Be sure and check for fingerprints   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> !<BR>Just kidding...<P>By the way, I know you said you replaced distributor stuff.  But apparently there are some less-than-high-quality ignition modules being sold.           <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 07-16-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000003||bcibob670||07-16-2000||06:59 PM||||thanks for the response...<BR>further info ...checked the ecm diagnostic no code stored ...once car shut down ecm diagnosis would not function (wouldnt blink 12)    The coil pack wasnt replaced in the distributor...the module was replaced...Ill have to check it out .   <P>thanks will keep you posted ...||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||07-17-2000||01:38 AM||||If I read your second post correctly you say that when the car shuts down you cannot get the ECM to show the normal code 12? If that is the case I would go to an electronics store and buy "Quick Freeze" It is a non conductive freeze spray to cool over heated parts for a few seconds to aid in troubleshooting. Then drive the car, When it shuts down, Spray the ECM case down for a few seconds. If car starts right away then the ECM is overheating. You can check the coil and distributor the same way. The only time I have seen the ECM not show code 12 is when it was bad or you have no power to it.<BR>Maybe something is getting overheated and loading down the electrical system. Easily verified, When car shuts down does the lights still work? My best guess is ECM.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
